Bootstrap: docker
From: rockylinux/rockylinux@sha256:8101994123cf3d0a8fee517bee7f39e555c7d92bd2d9eb3303cc988a0eeed00f

# =============================================================================
# bx_scgpt.1.0
#
#   Target scripts:
#     - scgpt_prep.1.0.py (AI-ready dataset; reads the scGPT vocabulary)
#     - scgpt_train.1.0.py (patient-level classifier on scGPT embeddings)
#     - scgpt_predict.1.0.py (patient probabilities with a trained model)
#     - scgpt_compare.1.0.py (models against baselines on the same folds)
#   scgpt_embed.1.0.py also runs here on the CPU (very slowly); it is
#   registered with the GPU image bx_scgpt_gpu.1.0, which differs from this one
#   only in the PyTorch build (CUDA 12.6).
#
#   [MANDATORY BUILD RULES]
#   1) Base OS must be Rocky Linux 9. Rocky 8 is not allowed: it causes
#      environment conflicts on the customer's servers.
#   2) Anaconda Inc. repositories are strictly prohibited. Specifically:
#        - repo.anaconda.com and the defaults / main / r / anaconda channels
#          are never used.
#        - The Anaconda Distribution and the Miniconda installer are never
#          used, because the Miniconda installer is served from
#          repo.anaconda.com and ships defaults-channel packages.
#      conda is therefore provided by Miniforge, which is published by the
#      conda-forge project on GitHub and defaults to the conda-forge channel.
#      Only conda-forge and bioconda (community channels, free-licence, merely
#      hosted on conda.anaconda.org) plus Rocky 9 BaseOS / AppStream are used.
#      Every conda command below passes --override-channels so that no channel
#      outside this list can ever be consulted.
#
#   [TOOL LOCATION]
#   This image installs its tools into the shared prefix /opt/bio (a conda
#   environment), so a tool that appears in more than one image resolves to
#   /opt/bio/bin/<tool>. /opt/bio/bin is first on PATH in %environment. The
#   supplied script calls its tools by name through PATH and needs no
#   change. Do not bind any host path over /opt/bio at runtime.
#
# [PURPOSE]
#   CPU modules of the CLOSHA scGPT patient-level prediction pipeline (scGPT,
#   Cui et al., Nature Methods 2024): building the AI-ready single-cell dataset
#   with patient labels and folds (scgpt_prep), training patient-level
#   classifiers on scGPT cell embeddings (scgpt_train), predicting new patients
#   (scgpt_predict), and comparing models with baselines (scgpt_compare).
#   The analysis scripts are supplied through CLOSHA's external bind paths,
#   rather than embedded in the image. This DEF provides their runtime and the
#   pretrained scGPT whole-human checkpoint.
#
# [INPUT AND OUTPUT STRUCTURE]
#   Inputs: per-sample count matrices (10x h5, h5ad, 10x mtx) with a sample
#   sheet, or the output folder of the previous module; outputs are per-module
#   folders as described in each script header.
#
# [REQUIRED RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES]
#   Python 3.11 with conda-forge pytorch 2.6.0 (CPU build, MKL),
#   torchtext 0.18.0 (scGPT's gene vocabulary class), scanpy 1.10.4, anndata
#   0.10.9, datasets 2.21.0 (imported by scGPT at start), numba, ipython,
#   seaborn, networkx, scikit-learn, h5py; scGPT 0.2.4 from its PyPI wheel
#   (not on conda-forge), installed with --no-deps from a SHA256-checked file.
#   scGPT's other declared dependencies (scvi-tools, scib, cell-gears, orbax,
#   wandb, flash-attn) are not imported by the pipeline scripts and are not
#   installed. The scGPT whole-human checkpoint (33 million human cells;
#   args.json, best_model.pt, vocab.json) is placed in
#   /opt/bio/share/scgpt/checkpoints/whole_human after SHA256 checks.
#   Runtime analysis needs no network access.
#
# [BASE IMAGE AND INSTALLATION RULES FOR THIS DEF]
#   Base image: the official Rocky Linux 9.8 image
#   rockylinux/rockylinux:9.8.20260525.0, pinned by its index digest in From.
#   conda: Miniforge3-26.7.2-0 (conda-forge project GitHub release),
#   SHA256-checked, used only during the build.
#   Packages: conda-forge only, with --override-channels and strict channel
#   priority. Top-level packages are pinned to exact versions; the full
#   resolved set with SHA256 values is recorded in
#   /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/conda-explicit.txt and checked so that every URL
#   is under conda.anaconda.org/conda-forge.
#   PyPI: one wheel (scgpt 0.2.4), fetched by URL and checked by SHA256,
#   installed with pip --no-deps --no-index (no index lookup).
#   scGPT checkpoint: fetched from the authors' public Google Drive folder by
#   fixed file IDs and checked by SHA256 (no separate licence is stated for
#   the weights; the scGPT repository is MIT).
#   Building requires internet access to github.com, conda.anaconda.org,
#   files.pythonhosted.org and drive.usercontent.google.com.
#   The conda solve needs about 1 GB of memory on top of the build.
#
# [HPC BUILD REQUIREMENTS]
#   Use a designated build environment and an unprivileged build account.
#   Do not run the build as the host root user or through sudo.
#   Do not bind production data or shared system directories into the build
#   container.
#   Host-side %pre and %setup sections are not used.
#   Package installation and cleanup run inside %post.
#   Build workspace: the fixed container-private path /__closha_build/work.
#   /__closha_build and the workspace are created with plain mkdir (no -p),
#   so the build stops if either already exists, including as a symlink or
#   a bind mount point. The Miniforge installer and its base installation,
#   the conda package cache (CONDA_PKGS_DIRS), the conda configuration file
#   (CONDARC), the downloaded wheel and checkpoint files, temporary files
#   (TMPDIR), and HOME for the install steps are all placed below it, so conda
#   itself does not remain in the image; only the /opt/bio environment does.
#   Cleanup removes only that exact workspace, after separate checks of the
#   path constant, symlinks, canonical path, and root-filesystem device, and
#   then removes the empty /__closha_build with rmdir. Any failed check stops
#   the build without deleting anything.
#   No wildcard cleanup of default paths is used.
#   These checks reduce accidents but do not prove host isolation. The build
#   system must keep host binds off /__closha_build, its parents, and children.
#   No broad cleanup of /tmp, /var/tmp, /root, or user home directories is used.
#   %test performs checks without creating or deleting data files.
#   The Apptainer output SIF, image cache, and build temporary files are host
#   artifacts; place them in an approved build workspace, separate from services.
#   This DEF does not clean the host's Apptainer cache or build workspace.
#   The build system must enforce account, workspace, and mount restrictions;
#   comments in this DEF cannot enforce the host's bind configuration.
#
# [RUNTIME ENVIRONMENT ISOLATION]
#   Apptainer binds the user's home directory by default. %environment puts
#   /opt/bio/bin first on a PATH without home entries and sets
#   PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1 so that packages in a host ~/.local are not imported.
#   The scripts send matplotlib, numba, Hugging Face and PyTorch caches to
#   output_dir/.cache (removed at the end) and set the Hugging Face libraries
#   offline, so nothing is written to the bound home.
#
# [CHANGES FROM THE PREVIOUS SIF]
#   No previous SIF or DEF; this is a new image (2026-11-03).
#   This is a new image; nothing existing is modified or overwritten.
#
# [VALIDATION STATUS AND REPRODUCIBILITY]
#   Checked: script imports, a solve of the package set from conda-forge only
#   (Miniforge conda), the Miniforge installer, wheel and checkpoint SHA256
#   values, and DEF %post and %test shell syntax (sh -n). The same package set
#   ran the five pipeline scripts on a small public PBMC test (Codespace,
#   2026-11-03); scgpt_embed matched scgpt.tasks.embed_data (cosine >= 0.997).
#   The tmpfs, mount check, workspace and cleanup blocks are copied unchanged
#   from bx_spatial.1.2.def (policy 1.7 sections 6 and 6.1), including the
#   python3 fallback for the fakeroot "Is a directory" cleanup failure.
#   Build from a shell without APPTAINER_* or SINGULARITY_* bind variables.
#   %test checks the Python and package versions, the CPU build of PyTorch,
#   the checkpoint checksums, and embeds two synthetic cells with the real
#   checkpoint.
#   Unresolved: the Miniforge and conda package recipe is not yet a
#   registered approved recipe; the PyPI wheel and Google Drive checkpoint are
#   new external assets.
#
# [CHANGELOG]
#   2026-11-03 / 1.0: Created for scgpt_prep, scgpt_train, scgpt_predict and
#   scgpt_compare 1.0, following policy 1.7.
# =============================================================================

%labels
    Maintainer       KOBIC Bio-Express
    Pipeline         scGPT
    Component        bx_scgpt
    Version          1.0
    BaseOS           RockyLinux9
    CondaInstaller   Miniforge3-26.7.2-0
    AnacondaRepoFree true
    scGPTVersion     0.2.4
    PyTorchVersion   2.6.0-cpu
    PythonVersion    3.11
    Description      "scGPT 0.2.4 pipeline CPU modules (PyTorch 2.6 CPU) with the whole-human checkpoint"

%environment
    export PATH=/opt/bio/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
    export LANG=C.UTF-8
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
    # Keep packages from a host-bound ~/.local out of this runtime.
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1

%post
    set -eu
    # Fixed container PATH; ignore any inherited PATH that may point into a
    # host-bound home directory.
    export PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8

    # Apptainer builds keep the admin "mount tmp = yes" setting, so %post sees
    # the host /tmp and /var/tmp. Cover both with private tmpfs mounts in this
    # container's mount namespace before any other command runs; the host
    # directories are then unreachable. If mounting fails, the build stops.
    mount -t tmpfs -o mode=1777,nosuid,nodev closha-tmp /tmp
    mount -t tmpfs -o mode=1777,nosuid,nodev closha-vartmp /var/tmp
    [ "$(stat -f -c %T /tmp)" = tmpfs ] || { echo "/tmp is not private" >&2; exit 1; }
    [ "$(stat -f -c %T /var/tmp)" = tmpfs ] || { echo "/var/tmp is not private" >&2; exit 1; }
    # With "mount dev = yes" the build may see the host's /dev and its shared
    # /dev/shm. Cover /dev/shm with a private tmpfs as well, then confirm from
    # the mount table that the newest /dev/shm mount is this one.
    if [ -d /dev/shm ]; then
        mount -t tmpfs -o mode=1777,nosuid,nodev closha-shm /dev/shm
        awk '$5 == "/dev/shm" { s = $0 } END { exit !(s ~ / - tmpfs closha-shm /) }' /proc/self/mountinfo ||
            { echo "/dev/shm is not private" >&2; exit 1; }
    fi
    # Record the mount table in the build log for review.
    cat /proc/self/mountinfo

    # Stop before any install step if the build container has a mount other
    # than those Apptainer creates for builds: the rootfs, /tmp and /var/tmp
    # (now covered above), private /etc/hosts and /etc/resolv.conf copies, and
    # kernel pseudo-filesystems under /dev, /proc, and /sys, plus, in builds
    # that run %post under the fakeroot command (no /etc/subuid entry), the
    # read-only fakeroot helpers Apptainer places under /.singularity.d/libs
    # (fakeroot, faked, libfakeroot.so, and getopt, which the fakeroot wrapper
    # script calls; getopt was seen in the 2026-10-14 production build log).
    # This rejects host paths added through APPTAINER_BINDPATH or
    # APPTAINER_MOUNT, which the build passes into %post from the caller's
    # environment.
    awk '
        { split($0, sep, " - "); split(sep[2], f, " "); mp = $5; opt = $6; fs = f[1]; src = $4 }
        mp == "/" || mp == "/tmp" || mp == "/var/tmp" { next }
        (mp == "/etc/hosts" || mp == "/etc/resolv.conf") &&
            src ~ /\/bundle-temp-[0-9]+\/(hosts|resolv\.conf)$/ { next }
        (mp == "/dev" || mp ~ /^\/dev\//) && fs ~ /^(devtmpfs|tmpfs|devpts|mqueue|hugetlbfs)$/ { next }
        (mp == "/proc" || mp ~ /^\/proc\//) && fs ~ /^(proc|binfmt_misc|autofs)$/ { next }
        (mp == "/sys" || mp ~ /^\/sys\//) &&
            fs ~ /^(sysfs|cgroup|cgroup2|securityfs|selinuxfs|debugfs|tracefs|bpf|fusectl|configfs|pstore|efivarfs)$/ { next }
        mp == "/.singularity.d/libs" && fs == "tmpfs" && opt ~ /^ro(,|$)/ { next }
        mp ~ /^\/\.singularity\.d\/libs\/(fakeroot|faked|libfakeroot\.so|getopt)$/ && opt ~ /^ro(,|$)/ { next }
        { print "Unexpected mount in build container: " mp " (" fs ")" > "/dev/stderr"; bad = 1 }
        END { exit bad }
    ' /proc/self/mountinfo || { echo "Build stopped: remove extra bind settings and rebuild" >&2; exit 1; }

    fail() { echo "Build check failed: $*" >&2; exit 1; }

    # Fixed container-private workspace from the approved profile.
    # Plain mkdir (no -p) fails if a path already exists, including a symlink
    # or a bind mount point, so the build stops instead of reusing it.
    BUILD_ROOT=/__closha_build
    WORK=/__closha_build/work
    mkdir -m 0700 -- "$BUILD_ROOT"
    mkdir -m 0700 -- "$WORK"
    mkdir -p -- "$WORK/src" "$WORK/tmp" "$WORK/home" "$WORK/conda-pkgs"

    # The admin config may bind the host home, and /tmp may be host-backed.
    # Redirect HOME, TMPDIR, and XDG directories into the workspace before any
    # other command runs, so no tool reads or writes host user files or /tmp.
    export HOME="$WORK/home"
    export TMPDIR="$WORK/tmp"
    export XDG_CACHE_HOME="$WORK/home/.cache"
    export XDG_CONFIG_HOME="$WORK/home/.config"
    export XDG_DATA_HOME="$WORK/home/.local/share"

    # The pinned conda packages below are for linux-64 only.
    [ "$(uname -m)" = x86_64 ] || fail "unsupported architecture"
    grep -q '^Rocky Linux release 9\.' /etc/rocky-release || fail "base OS is not Rocky Linux 9"

    # Miniforge from the conda-forge project's GitHub release (never Miniconda
    # or the Anaconda Distribution). SHA256 matches the published .sha256 file.
    # It is installed into the workspace and removed with it after use.
    curl --fail --location --retry 3 \
        --connect-timeout 30 --max-time 600 \
        --output "$WORK/src/Miniforge3-26.7.2-0-Linux-x86_64.sh" \
        https://github.com/conda-forge/miniforge/releases/download/26.7.2-0/Miniforge3-26.7.2-0-Linux-x86_64.sh
    printf '%s  %s\n' \
        281b0ac7d550802efc81af633225a5e6116d29ae72f3ab4eae7168c3931a4c05 \
        "$WORK/src/Miniforge3-26.7.2-0-Linux-x86_64.sh" | sha256sum --check -
    bash "$WORK/src/Miniforge3-26.7.2-0-Linux-x86_64.sh" -b -p "$WORK/miniforge"

    # conda configuration, package cache, and environments list stay in the
    # workspace. Only conda-forge is configured, defaults is empty, and every
    # command still passes --override-channels.
    export CONDARC="$WORK/condarc"
    export CONDA_PKGS_DIRS="$WORK/conda-pkgs"
    printf '%s\n' \
        'channels:' \
        '  - conda-forge' \
        'default_channels: []' \
        'channel_priority: strict' \
        'auto_update_conda: false' \
        'notify_outdated_conda: false' \
        'report_errors: false' \
        > "$CONDARC"
    CONDA="$WORK/miniforge/bin/conda"

    # Create the /opt/bio environment from exact package versions.
    # torchtext 0.18.0 is the last release and exists on conda-forge for
    # PyTorch up to 2.6, so PyTorch is 2.6.0 (CPU build).
    "$CONDA" create --yes --prefix /opt/bio \
        --override-channels --channel conda-forge \
        --strict-channel-priority \
        python=3.11.18 \
        'pytorch=2.6.0=cpu_mkl_py311_he84fd7d_104' \
        torchtext=0.18.0 \
        scanpy=1.10.4 \
        anndata=0.10.9 \
        datasets=2.21.0 \
        numpy=2.2.6 \
        pandas=2.3.3 \
        scipy=1.14.1 \
        scikit-learn=1.9.1 \
        numba=0.68.1 \
        matplotlib-base=3.10.9 \
        seaborn=0.13.2 \
        networkx=3.6.1 \
        h5py=3.16.0 \
        ipython=9.18.1 \
        pip

    # scGPT 0.2.4 is not on conda-forge: its PyPI wheel (pure Python) is fetched
    # by URL, checked by SHA256 and installed without dependencies or an index.
    curl --fail --location --retry 3 \
        --connect-timeout 30 --max-time 600 \
        --output "$WORK/src/scgpt-0.2.4-py3-none-any.whl" \
        https://files.pythonhosted.org/packages/py3/s/scgpt/scgpt-0.2.4-py3-none-any.whl
    printf '%s  %s\n' \
        aafa4ac8dcb5a3d7b81ab71f136baf324b7dc6d83109781340ba601fd236759f \
        "$WORK/src/scgpt-0.2.4-py3-none-any.whl" | sha256sum --check -
    PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1 PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1 /opt/bio/bin/python -m pip install \
        --no-deps --no-index "$WORK/src/scgpt-0.2.4-py3-none-any.whl"

    # scGPT whole-human checkpoint from the authors' Google Drive folder
    # (README "Pretrained scGPT checkpoints"), by fixed file IDs, SHA256-checked.
    CKPT=/opt/bio/share/scgpt/checkpoints/whole_human
    mkdir -p "$CKPT"
    for item in \
        "1hh2zGKyWAx3DyovD30GStZ3QlzmSqdk1 args.json c18e075e018140cb8b2d9029387b9de26607a5ce6a8ccabd6ead70cd76b95d60" \
        "1H3E_MJ-Dl36AQV6jLbna2EdvgPaqvqcC vocab.json acca93d114ca62c3f0f50debbd23e8c87f0714f4737764454f6b2b13f2e8580f" \
        "14AebJfGOUF047Eg40hk57HCtrb0fyDTm best_model.pt 6cb5d451ab5c4b33eb673adbe4fddc61d2389df1b89b7651a9fe2e557572b922"; do
        set -- $item
        curl --fail --location --retry 3 \
            --connect-timeout 30 --max-time 1800 \
            --output "$WORK/src/$2" \
            "https://drive.usercontent.google.com/download?id=$1&export=download&confirm=t"
        printf '%s  %s\n' "$3" "$WORK/src/$2" | sha256sum --check -
        cp -- "$WORK/src/$2" "$CKPT/$2"
    done
    chmod 0644 "$CKPT"/*
    ( cd "$CKPT" && sha256sum args.json best_model.pt vocab.json ) > /opt/bio/share/scgpt/checkpoints/whole_human.sha256

    # Record the exact package set with SHA256 values, then check that every
    # package came from conda-forge.
    mkdir -p /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt
    "$CONDA" list --prefix /opt/bio --explicit --sha256 > /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/conda-explicit.txt
    awk '
        /^https?:/ && $0 !~ /^https:\/\/conda\.anaconda\.org\/conda-forge\// { print "Package from a disallowed channel: " $0 > "/dev/stderr"; bad = 1 }
        END { exit bad }
    ' /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/conda-explicit.txt || fail "package from a disallowed channel"
    "$CONDA" list --prefix /opt/bio > /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/conda-packages.txt
    /opt/bio/bin/python -m pip list --format=freeze > /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/pip-freeze.txt
    printf '%s  %s\n' aafa4ac8dcb5a3d7b81ab71f136baf324b7dc6d83109781340ba601fd236759f \
        scgpt-0.2.4-py3-none-any.whl > /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/pypi-wheels.sha256
    cp /etc/rocky-release /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/base-os-release.txt
    rpm -qa --qf '%{NAME}\t%{VERSION}-%{RELEASE}\n' | sort > /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/rocky-packages.tsv
    /opt/bio/bin/python --version > /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/python-version.txt
    grep -F 'Python 3.11.18' /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/python-version.txt

    # Remove only the exact workspace created above; stop on any doubt.
    # Each check is a separate command so that set -e cannot skip a failure.
    [ "$WORK" = /__closha_build/work ] || fail "unexpected workspace path"
    [ ! -L "$BUILD_ROOT" ] || fail "parent is a symlink"
    [ ! -L "$WORK" ] || fail "workspace is a symlink"
    [ -d "$WORK" ] || fail "workspace is missing"
    [ "$(realpath -e -- "$WORK")" = "$WORK" ] || fail "canonical path changed"
    [ "$(stat -c %d -- "$WORK")" = "$(stat -c %d -- /)" ] || fail "workspace is on another filesystem"
    # Under the fakeroot command (production builds by an account without
    # /etc/subuid entries), a stale fakeroot record can report a directory as
    # a regular file. A production build showed this for miniforge/pkgs: rm
    # and Python's shutil.rmtree both called unlink on it and stopped with
    # "Is a directory". If rm fails, the rest is removed with the base
    # image's Python, which decides by the kernel's answer instead of stat:
    # it unlinks each entry and only on EISDIR opens it as a directory
    # (O_NOFOLLOW, same filesystem as the workspace), empties it, and removes
    # it with rmdir. Symbolic links are removed, never followed.
    if ! rm -rf --one-file-system -- "$WORK"; then
        echo "rm could not remove all of $WORK; retrying with python3" >&2
        /usr/bin/env python3 - "$WORK" <<'PYCLEAN' || fail "python3 could not remove the workspace"
import os, sys
# Remove the workspace without trusting file types from stat: under the
# fakeroot command a stale record can report a directory as a file. Each
# entry is unlinked; only when the kernel answers EISDIR is it opened as a
# directory (no symlink following, same device as the workspace), emptied,
# and removed with rmdir.
root = sys.argv[1]
dir_flags = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NOFOLLOW
root_fd = os.open(root, dir_flags)
root_dev = os.fstat(root_fd).st_dev
removed = [0, 0]

def empty(dir_fd, where):
    for name in os.listdir(dir_fd):
        try:
            os.unlink(name, dir_fd=dir_fd)
            removed[0] += 1
            continue
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue
        except IsADirectoryError:
            pass
        fd = os.open(name, dir_flags, dir_fd=dir_fd)
        try:
            if os.fstat(fd).st_dev != root_dev:
                sys.exit("not removing a different filesystem: %s/%s" % (where, name))
            empty(fd, where + "/" + name)
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
        os.rmdir(name, dir_fd=dir_fd)
        removed[1] += 1

try:
    empty(root_fd, root)
finally:
    os.close(root_fd)
os.rmdir(root)
print("python3 removed %d files and %d directories" % tuple(removed), file=sys.stderr)
PYCLEAN
    fi
    [ ! -e "$WORK" ] || fail "workspace could not be removed"
    rmdir -- "$BUILD_ROOT"

%runscript
    exec /opt/bio/bin/python3 "$@"

%test
    set -eu
    export PATH=/opt/bio/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1

    # The build runs %test separately from %post, again with the host /tmp and
    # /var/tmp bound (mount tmp = yes), and Python libraries create temporary
    # files there. When running as root (a root or fakeroot build), cover both,
    # and /dev/shm when present, with private tmpfs mounts first; if mounting
    # fails, the build stops.
    # Unprivileged runtime checks cannot mount and skip this step.
    if [ "$(id -u)" = 0 ]; then
        mount -t tmpfs -o mode=1777,nosuid,nodev closha-tmp /tmp
        mount -t tmpfs -o mode=1777,nosuid,nodev closha-vartmp /var/tmp
        [ "$(stat -f -c %T /tmp)" = tmpfs ] || { echo "/tmp is not private" >&2; exit 1; }
        [ "$(stat -f -c %T /var/tmp)" = tmpfs ] || { echo "/var/tmp is not private" >&2; exit 1; }
        if [ -d /dev/shm ]; then
            mount -t tmpfs -o mode=1777,nosuid,nodev closha-shm /dev/shm
            awk '$5 == "/dev/shm" { s = $0 } END { exit !(s ~ / - tmpfs closha-shm /) }' /proc/self/mountinfo ||
                { echo "/dev/shm is not private" >&2; exit 1; }
        fi
    fi

    for cmd in python3 nproc env; do
        command -v "$cmd" >/dev/null || { echo "missing command: $cmd" >&2; exit 1; }
    done
    [ "$(command -v python3)" = /opt/bio/bin/python3 ]
    python3 --version | grep -F 'Python 3.11.18'

    # Checkpoint files as fetched in %post.
    ( cd /opt/bio/share/scgpt/checkpoints/whole_human && sha256sum --check --quiet ../whole_human.sha256 )

    # Library caches of this check go to a private temporary folder, never home.
    T="$(mktemp -d)"
    MPLCONFIGDIR="$T/mpl" NUMBA_CACHE_DIR="$T/numba" XDG_CACHE_HOME="$T/cache" HF_HOME="$T/hf" \
    TORCH_HOME="$T/torch" HF_DATASETS_OFFLINE=1 HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1 python3 - <<'PYTEST'
import json, warnings
warnings.filterwarnings("ignore")
import numpy as np, torch, torchtext, scanpy, anndata, sklearn, matplotlib, scgpt
from importlib.metadata import version
want = {"torch": "2.6.0", "torchtext": "0.18.0", "scanpy": "1.10.4", "anndata": "0.10.9",
        "scgpt": "0.2.4", "datasets": "2.21.0", "numpy": "2.2.6", "scikit-learn": "1.9.1"}
for pkg, v in want.items():
    got = version(pkg)
    if not got.startswith(v):
        raise SystemExit(f"unexpected {pkg} version: {got}")
if torch.version.cuda is not None:
    raise SystemExit(f"PyTorch is not the CPU build: CUDA {torch.version.cuda}")
from scgpt.model import TransformerModel
from scgpt.tokenizer import GeneVocab
from scgpt.utils import load_pretrained
from scgpt.data_collator import DataCollator
ck = "/opt/bio/share/scgpt/checkpoints/whole_human"
vocab = GeneVocab.from_file(f"{ck}/vocab.json")
for t in ["<pad>", "<cls>", "<eoc>"]:
    if t not in vocab:
        vocab.append_token(t)
cfg = json.load(open(f"{ck}/args.json"))
m = TransformerModel(ntoken=len(vocab), d_model=cfg["embsize"], nhead=cfg["nheads"], d_hid=cfg["d_hid"],
                     nlayers=cfg["nlayers"], nlayers_cls=cfg["n_layers_cls"], n_cls=1, vocab=vocab,
                     dropout=cfg["dropout"], pad_token=cfg["pad_token"], pad_value=cfg["pad_value"],
                     do_mvc=True, do_dab=False, use_batch_labels=False, domain_spec_batchnorm=False,
                     explicit_zero_prob=False, use_fast_transformer=False, pre_norm=False)
load_pretrained(m, torch.load(f"{ck}/best_model.pt", map_location="cpu"), verbose=False)
m.eval()
# Two synthetic cells with 20 known genes each: the embedding must be finite and 512 long.
genes = ["CD3D", "CD3E", "IL7R", "LTB", "MALAT1", "RPL13", "RPS27", "TPT1", "EEF1A1", "B2M",
         "LYZ", "S100A8", "S100A9", "CD14", "FCN1", "VCAN", "CST3", "TYROBP", "FTL", "FTH1"]
ids = [vocab[g] for g in genes]
col = DataCollator(do_padding=True, pad_token_id=vocab["<pad>"], pad_value=cfg["pad_value"], do_mlm=False,
                   do_binning=True, max_length=1201, sampling=True, keep_first_n_tokens=1)
items = []
for k in range(2):
    vals = np.random.default_rng(k).integers(1, 20, len(ids)).astype(np.float32)
    items.append({"genes": torch.tensor([vocab["<cls>"]] + ids), "expressions": torch.tensor([cfg["pad_value"], *vals])})
d = col(items)
with torch.no_grad():
    h = m._encode(d["gene"], d["expr"], src_key_padding_mask=d["gene"].eq(vocab["<pad>"]))[:, 0, :]
if h.shape != (2, 512) or not torch.isfinite(h).all():
    raise SystemExit("scGPT embedding check failed")
print("scGPT", scgpt.__version__, "torch", torch.__version__, "(CPU build)")
PYTEST
    rm -rf -- "$T"

    # Every recorded package URL must come from conda-forge.
    awk '
        /^https?:/ && $0 !~ /^https:\/\/conda\.anaconda\.org\/conda-forge\// { print "Package from a disallowed channel: " $0 > "/dev/stderr"; bad = 1 }
        END { exit bad }
    ' /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/conda-explicit.txt
    [ "$(grep -c -E '^https://' /opt/bio/share/bx-scgpt/conda-explicit.txt)" -gt 0 ]

    # The build workspace must not remain in the image.
    if [ -e /__closha_build ]; then
        echo 'Build workspace /__closha_build remains in the image' >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    echo 'scGPT 0.2.4 / PyTorch 2.6.0 CPU: bx_scgpt runtime dependency checks passed.'

%help
    CLOSHA scGPT CPU environment: Python 3.11, PyTorch 2.6.0 (CPU),
    scGPT 0.2.4 with the whole-human checkpoint in
    /opt/bio/share/scgpt/checkpoints/whole_human, scanpy, anndata.
    Base OS: Rocky Linux 9. Tools are installed in /opt/bio from conda-forge with
    Miniforge (no Anaconda Inc. repository or Miniconda); scGPT from its
    SHA256-checked PyPI wheel.

    The CLOSHA build system builds this DEF with its fixed Slurm job (policy
    section 6.2), on an internet-connected x86_64 node, without extra binds:
        apptainer build bx_scgpt.1.0.sif bx_scgpt.1.0.def
    It must pass both as root and with fakeroot.

    Store the SIF, Apptainer cache, and build temporary files in an approved host
    build workspace. APPTAINER_CACHEDIR and APPTAINER_TMPDIR select the cache and
    temporary directories. Container cleanup must never target host-backed binds.
    Build from a shell without APPTAINER_* or SINGULARITY_* bind variables.

    Check the image:
        apptainer test --cleanenv bx_scgpt.1.0.sif

    The scripts are supplied externally through approved runtime binds, e.g.:
        apptainer exec bx_scgpt.1.0.sif python3 scgpt_prep.1.0.py key=value ...
    The image holds no data besides the scGPT checkpoint. Writable runtime binds
    can expose host data to the analysis scripts.
    Runtime analysis requires no network access.
